WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.260
SETH VARGO: Hey, everyone,
and welcome back.

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:03.510
Today we have Jaice with us.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:07.110
And we're here to talk about
the community, diversity

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:09.395
and inclusion efforts, and
how Google's giving back.

00:00:09.395 --> 00:00:10.650
Jaice, why don't you
introduce yourself.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.100
JAICE SINGER DUMARS:
Thinks, Seth.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:13.290
So I'm Jaice Singer DuMars.

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:17.460
I work in cloud
native strategy, and I

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.440
worked for Sara
Novotny, who is one

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.800
of the seminal members of
the Kubernetes community

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.350
who helped create what we think
of as a Kubernetes community

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:25.849
now.

00:00:25.849 --> 00:00:27.120
So a lot of those things.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.130
So basically I spent
every day learning

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.020
about all the amazing
things she does.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.817
And so that's where I
start from, is that spot.

00:00:33.817 --> 00:00:34.650
SETH VARGO: Awesome.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:36.720
So aside from Sara,
what's your favorite part

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.780
about the Kubernetes community?

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.190
JAICE SINGER DUMARS: What I've
heard from a lot of people

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:44.730
is that this is one of the
best open source Kubernetes

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:49.830
to come in as a complete
beginner even to coding

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:52.080
or to community
itself and to feel

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.440
like you have a home,
that people care

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.290
about your progression in the
community and all the things

00:00:58.290 --> 00:00:59.460
that you care about as well.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.460
SETH VARGO: What are some
things that Kubernetes

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:04.170
is doing to help
advance equality,

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:05.670
help expand diversity,
and make sure

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:07.576
that it's an
inclusive environment?

00:01:07.576 --> 00:01:09.480
JAICE SINGER DUMARS:
Well, this year

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:14.010
we bootstrapped the Kubernetes
Code of Conduct Committee.

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.080
So we actually have a
group of five people who

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:18.780
are focused on making
sure that those things are

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.450
evident across the
community, as well as having

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:24.220
really strong policies and
processes in place around that.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:25.470
So that's really great.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:26.640
I'm one of the five people.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.310
Paris Pitman from
Google is also on there.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:31.590
So that's great we have
Google representation on that.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:32.280
SETH VARGO: Awesome.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.488
It sounds like there's a
lot of Google representation

00:01:34.488 --> 00:01:36.540
and Kubernetes obviously
on the technical side,

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:38.249
but also from the
community side as well.

00:01:38.249 --> 00:01:39.623
What are some
other ways in which

00:01:39.623 --> 00:01:41.840
Google is getting
involved and helping out?

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.850
JAICE SINGER DUMARS: Well, we've
helped the mentorship program

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:46.830
move forward, so
that is a great entry

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.060
point for a lot of
people who want to get

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:49.143
involved in the community.

00:01:49.143 --> 00:01:51.510
And then again, Paris
Pitman is a dynamo.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:54.630
She does so much work for
the contributor experience

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:55.980
special interest group.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:59.564
And basically we are
constantly seeking out

00:01:59.564 --> 00:02:01.230
opportunities where
we can widen the net

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:03.397
and bring more people
into the community.

00:02:03.397 --> 00:02:04.230
SETH VARGO: Awesome.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:05.160
So one last question.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:05.370
JAICE SINGER DUMARS: Sure.

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:07.453
SETH VARGO: If you could
share one piece of advice

00:02:07.453 --> 00:02:08.904
to someone new to
the community--

00:02:08.904 --> 00:02:10.320
maybe it's a student
or maybe it's

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:11.340
someone who's been
in the industry

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:13.890
for 50 years-- what's one thing
that you would say to them?

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:15.514
JAICE SINGER DUMARS:
I'd say that don't

00:02:15.514 --> 00:02:18.870
be afraid to talk to
anybody in the project.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.690
If somebody is seen as a
luminary like Tim Hawkin

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:24.060
or Brian Grant or
the thought leaders,

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:25.486
they're completely approachable.

00:02:25.486 --> 00:02:27.360
And everybody in the
project is the same way.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.730
So if you have a question,
it doesn't matter who it is.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:31.500
This is a community where
you could ask it and get

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:32.587
it answered in a good way.

00:02:32.587 --> 00:02:33.420
SETH VARGO: Awesome.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:34.540
Thank you very much, Jaice.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:35.190
JAICE SINGER DUMARS:
My pleasure, Seth.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:36.440
Thanks

